<p>Trade ministers wrapped up a four-day World Trade Organization meeting in Yaoundé, Cameroon, without a final deal to extend the moratorium on customs duties for electronic transmissions or to approve a broader WTO reform work program. That leaves the tariff freeze set to expire on March 30. The immediate dispute was over how long the extension should last. Brazil favored a shorter renewal, while the United States pushed for a much longer one, or even a permanent arrangement. That gap helped sink the broader package.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/YO_-1OVNoTy3okHt9UdPfSX9EeDvXbW8eUR8o0V_6HY06qOR1yr6_to3j5f2nIqS0a8js6RskjJyRcrzEVIIZcNvFBDsRiy71-mNO0MLvzMdjQqwazBr8JSUWJSZ7y3ee0CuyY5e735th2qi3zzNRHDvmrxZELjcn_8' alt='WTO ministerial talks in Cameroon ended without a final deal on e-commerce duties.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Near compromise: Officials said a compromise had been taking shape around a four-year extension plus roughly a one-year sunset clause, which diplomats said would have kept the moratorium in place until 2031 before the talks fell apart.</li><li>US linkage: Politico reported that U.S. officials tied any moratorium extension to agreement on the broader WTO reform package, adding pressure in the final stretch of negotiations.</li><li>Main deliverable missed: The reform work plan was supposed to be the meeting&#x27;s main deliverable, so the fight over e-commerce duties derailed more than a sector-specific decision.</li><li>Overtime finish: Ministers negotiated past the planned close and into the early hours of Monday before Director-General Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala called time as delegations began leaving Yaoundé.</li></ul><blockquote>We have run out of time. Some have already caught flights, and some have changed flights, and some will need to go soon. - Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Brazil: Brazil pushed back against extending the moratorium at all by the end of the meeting after earlier seeking only a two-year extension, underscoring its preference for a shorter commitment than other members wanted. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/wto-talks-on-e-commerce-duties-moratorium-deadlocked-as-reform-plan-nears-deal'>Straits Times</a>)</li><li>United States: U.S. negotiators pushed for a much longer horizon, with reports saying Washington wanted either a permanent extension or a 10-year commitment for the e-commerce moratorium. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/wto-meeting-ends-with-no-deal/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li><li>WTO leadership: Director-General Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala urged members to use the draft texts developed in Yaoundé as a starting point for post-ministerial talks in Geneva on unresolved issues. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/wto-meeting-ends-with-no-deal/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li><li>United Kingdom: Trade Secretary Peter Kyle said the UK had pushed for WTO changes and called the lack of a collective decision a setback for global trade. (<a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/wto-talks-end-in-deadlock-after-brazil-blocks-deal/'>Michael West</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/ac711230-3ee6-467f-824a-3a74d356076c'>WTO reform talks collapse over US-Brazil stand-off</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/wto-talks-end-in-stalemate-over-e-commerce/a-76587532'>WTO talks end in stalemate over e-commerce</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/wto-talks-end-in-deadlock-after-brazil-blocks-deal/'>WTO talks end in deadlock after Brazil blocks deal</a> - michaelwest.com.au</li><li><a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/wto-meeting-ends-with-no-deal/'>WTO meeting ends with no deal</a> - politico.eu</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/wto-talks-on-e-commerce-duties-moratorium-deadlocked-as-reform-plan-nears-deal'>WTO talks end in deadlock after Brazil blocks deal on e-commerce duties</a> - straitstimes.com</li></ul>